          "text": "{\"schema_version\":\"ai-evaluator-v2/1.1\",\"case_id\":\"1-1\",\"candidate_id\":\"candidate_3\",\"action_assessment\":{\"status\":\"preserved\",\"summary\":\"Most source activities are present with acceptable labels, including receive/accept/reject, inform, prepare, assemble, ship, and the storehouse handling steps.\"},\"flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"The graph allows assemble and ship to occur before storehouse completion, and it also imposes reserve -> back-order ordering between actions that should be conditional alternatives rather than a required sequence.\"},\"control_flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"Accepted-order control logic is incorrect: reserve vs. back-order is not modeled as a choice, the per-item repetition over the part list is missing, and synchronization is misplaced so assembly/shipping occur before the storehouse branch is completed.\"},\"main_error_type\":\"mixed_or_cascading\",\"explanation\":\"The source requires, after acceptance, parallel work between storehouse processing and engineering preparation, with assembly only after both preparation is finished and every part has been reserved or back-ordered; it also requires an availability-based choice between reserve and back-order repeated for each item. In the generated graph, the storehouse branch sequentially performs reserve then back-order, has no loop over part-list items, and the engineering branch proceeds from preparation directly to assembly, shipping, and finish before the join. This changes the core accepted-order behavior and permits success-path actions too early, so overall semantic preservation is substantially reduced despite many correct action labels.\",\"overall_score\":0.41,\"severity\":\"major\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"ambiguity_flag\":false,\"ambiguity_explanation\":\"\",\"requires_human_review\":false,\"review_reason\":\"\"}"
        }
